  "notes": "Source: University of Memphis Department of Computer Science official 'Suggested Four-Year Plan: General Concentration, Effective Fall 2025' PDF (https://preview.memphis.edu/cs/pdfs/forms_4yr_plan_general_f25.pdf), linked from the department's official programs page https://www.memphis.edu/cs/programs/. This is the genuine official term-by-term sequence for the BS in Computer Science, General Concentration (the department's three concentrations are General, Cybersecurity, and Artificial Intelligence and Data Science). Catalog year set to 2025-2026, the catalog year the 'Effective Fall 2025' edition belongs to. Transcription method: direct transcription of the plan's course codes, credit hours, and semester placement from the official PDF text; course titles taken from the University of Memphis Undergraduate Catalog course descriptions and the BS program core-course list (catalog.memphis.edu), e.g. COMP 3081 'CS3 Introduction to Software Design', COMP 4517 'Introduction to Human Computer Interaction'. Caveats: (1) The department's programs page also lists 'Effective Fall 2026' four-year-plan documents, but those PDF URLs did not resolve through public web tools as of 2026-09-30, so this plan uses the latest fully retrievable official edition (Fall 2025). The 2026-27 catalog program listing retains the same General Concentration core courses. (2) Prereq fields include only prerequisites explicitly published in official UofM course syllabi: COMP 2150 requires COMP 1900 and MATH 1910; COMP 3081 requires COMP 2150; COMP 4517 requires COMP 2150; COMP 4270 requires COMP 2150. COMP 1900 lists MATH 1910 as a prerequisite OR corequisite and the official plan schedules both in Fall Year 1, so it is not encoded as a strict prereq. (3) COMP 3410 is 3 credit hours in this edition (the Fall 2025 general, cybersecurity, data science, and AI plans all show 3 credits); semester totals then sum to exactly 120. (4) 'COMP elective' and 'Open elective' slots are student-choice slots; 'COMP elective' refers to the major's upper-division COMP electives per department advising. (5) Per UofM catalog rules, 42 upper-division hours are required for graduation; students should choose electives with advisor guidance to meet that rule.",
